---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SpreadsheetLab from "../../components/guides/SpreadsheetLab";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<BaseLayout title="Transposition in Excel and Google Sheets">
  <header class="wrap page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">GUIDE 01 / BUILD</p>
    <h1>
      Move columns.
      <br />
      Reveal a message<span class="accent">.</span>
    </h1>
    <p>
      Two small lessons for Excel and Google Sheets. Finish the five-column puzzle first; save the
      reusable solver for another day.
    </p>
    <div class="guide-meta">
      <span>AGES 11–14</span>
      <span>EXCEL 2019+ / GOOGLE SHEETS</span>
    </div>
  </header>
  <div class="wrap article-layout">
    <nav class="article-toc" aria-label="Guide contents">
      <p class="eyebrow">TWO SHORT LESSONS</p>
      <a href="#five">01 / Just five columns</a>
      <a href="#formula">02 / Read one letter</a>
      <a href="#reorder">03 / Try a new order</a>
      <a href="#reusable">04 / A reusable solver</a>
      <a href="#checks">05 / Check your sheet</a>
    </nav>
    <article class="guide-content">
      <h2 id="five">Lesson 1: just five columns</h2>
      <p>
        A transposition moves letters without changing them. This puzzle moves the same five columns
        in every row. It is a <strong>block transposition</strong>: read each row across. A cipher
        written down columns needs a different method.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Open a blank spreadsheet. Rename the first tab <strong>Input</strong>. Use the + beside
          the tabs to add two more sheets called <strong>Grid</strong> and <strong>Solve</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          On Input, select A1 and type <code>Ciphertext</code>. In A2 paste this original practice
          message: <code>SEMTL LVEEO RTSET</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          In Input!A3 enter <code>=SUBSTITUTE(A2," ","")</code>. The answer should be{" "}
          <code>SEMTLLVEEORTSET</code> (15 letters). We remove display spaces before counting
          positions.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Our message was made from “LET’S MOVE LETTERS”. No competition ciphertext is used. To type a
        formula, select its cell, type the whole formula beginning with <code>=</code>, and press
        Enter.
      </p>
      <h2 id="formula">Read one letter, then copy down</h2>
      <p>
        In the Grid tab, put these five formulas into A1 through E1.{" "}
        <code>MID(text,start,how many)</code> reads one character. <code>ROW()</code> is the current
        row number.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <caption>Five starting formulas on Grid</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Cell</th>
              <th>Formula</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {[1, 2, 3, 4, 5].map((n, i) => (
              <tr>
                <th>{"ABCDE"[i]}1</th>
                <td>
                  <code>{`=MID(Input!$A$3,${n}+(ROW()-1)*5,1)`}</code>
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Select A1:E1. Copy (Ctrl+C), select A2:E3 using the name box to the left of the formula bar,
        then paste (Ctrl+V). You should have three rows: <code>S E M T L</code>,{" "}
        <code>L V E E O</code>, <code>R T S E T</code>. On a Mac use Command instead of Ctrl.
      </p>
      <p>
        In row 1, the first formula reads position 1. In row 2 it reads 1 + 5 = 6. The{" "}
        <code>$A$3</code> stays fixed while the row number changes. Stop here and explain that to a
        partner before continuing.
      </p>
      <h2 id="reorder">Try a new column order</h2>
      <p>
        On Solve, use the same five formulas, but change the starting numbers to{" "}
        <strong>5, 2, 4, 1, 3</strong>. Copy the first row down to row 3. This reads Grid column 5,
        then 2, then 4, then 1, then 3.
      </p>
      <pre>=MID(Input!$A$3,5+(ROW()-1)*5,1)</pre>
      <p>
        That is Solve!A1. Solve!B1 starts at 2, C1 at 4, D1 at 1 and E1 at 3. Try other orders in
        the model below, then find the order that produces <code>LETSM / OVELE / TTERS</code>.
      </p>
      <SpreadsheetLab client:load />
      <p>
        Join one row at a time: in Solve!F1 enter <code>=TEXTJOIN("",TRUE,A1:E1)</code> and copy
        down to F3. In Solve!F5 enter <code>=TEXTJOIN("",TRUE,F1:F3)</code>. You should see{" "}
        <code>LETSMOVELETTERS</code>. Row helpers ensure the reading order is explicit in both
        spreadsheet apps.
      </p>
      <aside class="guide-callout">
        <strong>Good stopping point.</strong> You have built a working five-column decoder. Try a
        second message of the same width before starting Lesson 2.
      </aside>
      <h2 id="reusable">Lesson 2: change the width, keep the formulas</h2>
      <p>
        Add a new tab called <strong>Solver</strong>. This version handles widths from 2 to 20 and
        up to 100 rows (100 × width letters). It reads reordered rows; it does not reconstruct a
        columnar cipher.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Put <code>Width</code> in A1 and <code>5</code> in B1. Choose a whole number from 2 to 20.
        </li>
        <li>
          Put <code>Letters only</code> in A2 and <code>SEMTLLVEEORTSET</code> in B2. Use only A–Z:
          remove spaces, line breaks, punctuation and digits before pasting. <code>SUBSTITUTE</code>{" "}
          above removes ordinary spaces only.
        </li>
        <li>
          In A5:E5 enter the numbers <code>5 2 4 1 3</code> in separate cells. For a different
          width, use each number 1 to your width exactly once in A5 onwards. Entries beyond the
          selected width are ignored.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>Put this formula in A6:</p>
      <pre>=IF(COLUMN()&gt;$B$1,"",MID($B$2,A$5+(ROW()-6)*$B$1,1))</pre>
      <p>
        Copy A6, select A6:T105 using the name box, and paste. The formula now fills a 20-column,
        100-row workspace. It leaves columns beyond your width blank. MID returns an empty string
        when it reaches the end, so a short final row needs no padding.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <caption>What each part does at A6</caption>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th>
                <code>COLUMN()</code>
              </th>
              <td>1 in column A, 2 in B, …, 20 in T. IF hides unused columns.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>
                <code>A$5</code>
              </th>
              <td>
                The chosen source column. It becomes B$5 across, but stays on row 5 when copied
                down.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>
                <code>ROW()-6</code>
              </th>
              <td>0 in row 6, 1 in row 7, …; multiply by the width to skip complete blocks.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>
                <code>$B$1</code> / <code>$B$2</code>
              </th>
              <td>Fixed width and ciphertext cells. They never move when you copy.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        In U6 enter <code>=TEXTJOIN("",TRUE,A6:T6)</code>. Copy U6 down to U105. In U2 enter{" "}
        <code>=TEXTJOIN("",TRUE,U6:U105)</code>. U2 is your answer. Change B1, B2 and the active
        numbers in row 5 to try another puzzle.
      </p>
      <p>
        In U3 enter <code>=IF(LEN(B2)&gt;100*B1,"Add more rows","All letters fit")</code>. For a
        longer message, extend both the grid and row helpers, then extend the range in U2. The
        warning reminds you when 100 rows are insufficient.
      </p>
      <h2 id="checks">Check your sheet before trusting an answer</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Width 5, key 5 2 4 1 3, the original message: U2 must be <code>LETSMOVELETTERS</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Width 3, key 3 1 2, message <code>ABCDEFGH</code>: U2 must be <code>CABFDEGH</code>. Empty
          cells in the final row are skipped.
        </li>
        <li>
          Width 20, key 20 19 … 1, message <code>ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRST</code>: the answer is{" "}
          <code>TSRQPONMLKJIHGFEDCBA</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Each source column must appear once in the active key cells. A repeated or missing number
          repeats or loses letters. Check that the output length equals the input length.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <details>
        <summary>Something looks wrong?</summary>
        <p>
          If a formula is shown as text, remove any leading apostrophe and set the cell format to
          General/Automatic, then re-enter it. Some spreadsheet locales use semicolons instead of
          commas between formula arguments. Keep the empty quotes. If copied formulas all show the
          same row, check that ROW has parentheses and that only the intended references contain $.
        </p>
      </details>
      <p class="small">
        The fixed-width teaching sequence adapts the formula structure in the teacher-provided Wilf
        workbook; its ciphertext is not reproduced. Function references:{" "}
        <a href="https://support.google.com/docs/table/25273?hl=en">Google Sheets function list</a>,{" "}
        <a href="https://support.google.com/docs/answer/7013992?hl=en">Google TEXTJOIN</a> and{" "}
        <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/mid-function-d5f9e25c-d7d6-472e-b568-4ecb12433028">
          Microsoft MID
        </a>
        . No dynamic array or spill reference is required.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href={`${base}/tools/#transposition`}>
          Open the transposition tool ↗
        </a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
